# Requests reach this host two ways and both have to resolve to the real visitor address: straight
# to the DuckDNS name, or through the CloudFront distribution that fronts the SPA and routes
# /api/* here. CloudFront appends the viewer address to X-Forwarded-For, and Caddy would then
# append CloudFront's own edge address on top -- leaving the edge as the right-most entry.
# RemoteIpValve resolves the right-most untrusted hop, so every visitor arriving through the CDN
# would land on the same identity. The /api/v1/auth/ throttle counts refresh as well as login at
# 10/minute, so that is not a brute-force edge case: a few ordinary users refreshing tokens would
# lock each other out. Naming CloudFront's origin-facing ranges as trusted proxies makes
# {client_ip} skip them and land on the viewer, for CDN and direct traffic alike.
#
# Spoofing stays impossible on both paths: a direct caller is not a trusted proxy, so its own
# connection address wins over any X-Forwarded-For it invents, and a viewer's invented header sits
# to the LEFT of the address CloudFront appends.
#
# These ranges are AWS's and they do change. Refresh them with:
#   curl -s https://ip-ranges.amazonaws.com/ip-ranges.json | python3 -c "import json,sys; d=json.load(sys.stdin); print(' '.join(sorted({p['ip_prefix'] for p in d['prefixes'] if p['service']=='CLOUDFRONT_ORIGIN_FACING'} ) + sorted({p['ipv6_prefix'] for p in d['ipv6_prefixes'] if p['service']=='CLOUDFRONT_ORIGIN_FACING'})))"
# Last refreshed 2026-09-10 from syncToken 1789026425. A stale list degrades to edge-keyed
# throttling for the new range only -- it never opens a spoofing hole.
{
	servers {
		trusted_proxies static 13.124.199.0/24 130.176.0.0/18 130.176.128.0/21 130.176.136.0/23 130.176.140.0/22 130.176.144.0/20 130.176.160.0/19 130.176.192.0/19 130.176.64.0/21 130.176.72.0/22 130.176.76.0/24 130.176.78.0/23 130.176.80.0/22 130.176.86.0/23 130.176.88.0/21 130.176.96.0/19 15.158.0.0/16 18.68.0.0/16 204.246.166.0/24 205.251.218.0/24 24.110.128.0/17 3.172.0.0/18 3.172.64.0/18 3.29.57.0/26 52.46.0.0/18 52.82.128.0/23 52.82.134.0/23 54.182.128.0/20 54.182.144.0/21 54.182.154.0/23 54.182.156.0/22 54.182.160.0/21 54.182.172.0/22 54.182.176.0/21 54.182.184.0/22 54.182.188.0/23 54.182.224.0/21 54.182.240.0/21 54.182.248.0/22 54.239.134.0/23 54.239.170.0/23 54.239.204.0/22 54.239.208.0/21 64.252.128.0/18 64.252.64.0/18 70.132.0.0/18 2600:9000:1000::/36 2600:9000:5200::/40 2600:9000:6000::/36
	}
}

# SITE_ADDRESS is the DuckDNS hostname. Naming it here (rather than using :443) is what lets
# Caddy obtain and renew a Let's Encrypt certificate automatically over HTTP-01.
{$SITE_ADDRESS} {
	encode zstd gzip

	# Overwrite rather than append: the app must see exactly one address, the one Caddy resolved
	# against the trusted-proxy list above. Tomcat's RemoteIpValve (server.forward-headers-strategy:
	# native) trusts Caddy because its container address is inside the docker bridge range, so this
	# single entry becomes getRemoteAddr(). That is what makes the login throttle count real
	# clients, and what makes HSTS actually emit.
	reverse_proxy app:8080 {
		header_up X-Forwarded-For {client_ip}

		health_uri /actuator/health
		health_interval 30s
		health_timeout 5s
	}

	log {
		output stdout
		format console
	}
}
